## ABSTRACT
The suitable materials, metal nitrides, are a promising class of electrocatalyst materials for a highly efficient oxygen evolution reaction (OER) because they exhibit superior intrinsic conductivity and have higher sustainability than oxide-based materials. To our knowledge, for the first time, we report a designable synthesis of three-dimensional (3D) and mesoporous \(\mathsf{C o}_{3}\mathsf{N}@\mathsf{a}\) morphous N-doped carbon (AN-C) nanocubes (NCs) with well-controlled open-framework structures via monodispersed \(\mathsf{C o}_{3}[\mathsf{C o}(\mathsf{C N})_{6}]_{2}\) Prussian blue analogue (PBA) NC precursors using in situ nitridation and calcination processes. \(\mathsf{C o}_{3}\mathsf{N}@\mathsf{A N-C}\) NCs (2 h) demonstrate better OER activity with a remarkably low Tafel plot \((69.6\;\mathsf{m V}\!\cdot\!\mathsf{d e c}^{-1})\), low overpotential of \(280\;\mathsf{m V}\) at a current density of \(10\;\mathsf{m A}\!\cdot\!\mathsf{c m}^{-1}\). Additionally, excellent cycling stability in alkaline electrolytes was exhibited without morphological changes and voltage elevations, superior to most reported hierarchical structures of transition-metal nitride particles. The presented strategy for synergy effects of metal-organic frameworks (MOFs)-derived transition-metal nitrides-carbon hybrid nanostructures provides prospects for developing high-performance and advanced electrocatalyst materials.
## KEYWORDS
transition-metal nitride, metal organic framework, mesoporous, oxygen evaluation reaction, alkaline water electrolysis
## 1 Introduction
In recent years, electrocatalytic and photocatalytic water splitting, as a next-generation energy conversion technology for producing hydrogen and oxygen, has been widely considered for solving energy and environmental problems [1, 2]. However, the half reaction of oxygen evolution reaction (OER) plays a critical role in improving overall conversion efficiency of the water-splitting process, because the OER system encounters the bottleneck of a kinetically sluggish reaction with multistep proton-coupled electron transfer and considerable overpotential value (> thermodynamic value: 1.23 V) [3, 4].
Precious oxide materials ( \(\mathrm{RuO}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{IrO}_{2}\) ) as a superior active catalyst for OER have been used; however, the high cost, poor stability, and scarcity of these metals hinder widespread practical and technical use [5, 6]. Hence, extensive research has focused on inexpensive earth-abundant metal-based materials, such as multicomposite transition-metal (Ni, Fe, Mn, Co, etc.) compounds (nitride, oxide, hydroxide, and phosphide), as well as hybrid structures with carbon-based materials (graphite, graphene, and carbon nanotubes (CNT)) as candidate catalysts for development of OER electrocatalyst [7–10].
Among them, Co-based nanostructures with 3D electronic configuration, such as morphology-engineered \({\mathrm{Co}}_{3}{\mathrm{O}}_{4}\) and their composites
Among them, Co-based nanostructures with 3D electronic configuration, such as morphology-engineered \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) and their composites with carbon materials (N-doped graphene/\(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) and Co/N-doped CNT) have been studied for reducing overpotential and improving stability in electrochemical oxidation of water because of large specific reaction sites and synergistic electrical coupling effect [11–14]. For most Co-based materials (semiconductors and insulators), their poor intrinsic conductivity limits efficient OER performance. Surprisingly, metal nitrides (Co-Mo-N, \(\mathrm{Ni}_{2}\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{N}\), \(\mathrm{Ni}_{3}\mathrm{N}\), and \(\mathrm{Co}_{x}\mathrm{N}\)) with metallic properties reveal high electrocatalytic abilities towards HER and OER because of their superior intrinsic conductivity [15–17]. Recently, Wu and co-workers reported that porous \(\mathrm{Co}_{4}\mathrm{N}\) and \(\mathrm{Ni}_{3}\mathrm{N}\) nanostructures on supporting electrodes by ammonia gas annealing exhibited excellent OER performance [18, 19]. Despite promising OER performance, one of the challenging issues for improving electrolytic property is thus control of morphology and shape, including highly exposed reaction sites, surface permeability, and better cycling performance.
Encapsulation of a carbon materials shell is a promising technique for preventing corrosion and aggregation of active materials during electrochemical reactions. The nitrogen-doped carbon-shell coated hybrid structure could allow improved conductivity, charge transfer efficiency, and chemical stability, providing effective improvement in electrocatalytic performance [20].
Herein, we report a successful design and fabrication of unique
mesoporous \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{N}@\mathrm{amorphous}\) N-doped carbon (AN-C) nanocubes (NCs) based on in situ nitridation and calcination via a Prussian blue analogue (PBA) of \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}[\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{CN})_{6}]_{2}\) NC precursors. Unique architectures (mesoporous and AN-C hybrid) could assign well-dispersed pores, highly active sites, and good durability of \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{N}\) NCs for OER. A mesoporous \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{N}@\mathrm{AN-C}\) NCs electrode exhibits outstanding performance with a low overpotential of \(280~\mathrm{mV}\) at \(10\;\mathrm{mA}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}\) . Tafel slope of \(69.6\;\mathrm{mV}{\cdot}\mathrm{dec}^{-1}\) , the largest \(C_{\mathrm{dl}}\) \((39\,\mathrm{mF}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2})\) , as well as excellent stability at \(20\;\mathrm{mA}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}\) in alkaline (1 M KOH) solution for \(24\,\mathrm{{h}}\) .
## 2 Experimental
2.1 Synthesis of \(\mathbf{Co}_{3}[\mathbf{Co}(\mathbf{CN})_{6}]_{2}\) Prussian blue analogue (PBA) nanocube (NC) precursors and mesoporous \(\mathbf{Co}_{3}\mathbf{N}@\mathbf{amorphous}\) N-doped carbon (AN-C) NCs
We synthesized uniform \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}[\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{CN})_{6}]_{2}\) PBA NC precursors by modifying an established procedure [21]. In a typical synthesis, we dissolved \(0.1374\,\mathrm{mmol}\) of cobalt (II) acetate tetrahydrate (reagent grade, \(\mathrm{(CH_{3}C O O)_{2}C o_{4}H_{2}O,}\) Sigma-Aldrich, USA), \(0.7174\ \mathrm{mmol}\) of sodium dodecylbenzenesulfonate (SDBS) (technical grade, \(\mathrm{C_{18}H_{29}N a O_{3}S,}\) Sigma-Aldrich, USA) in \(25~\mathrm{mL}\) of D. I. water to form solution A. We dissolved \(0.1502\,\mathrm{\mmol}\) of potassium hexacyanoferrate (III) (ACS reagent, \(\geq99.0\%\) , \(\mathrm{K}_{3}[\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{CN})_{6}]\) , Sigma-Aldrich, USA) in \(25~\mathrm{mL}\) of D. I. water to form solution B. Solutions A and B were heated at \(55~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) in an oil bath with stirring for \(1\;\mathrm{{h}}\) . Then solutions A and B were mixed and stirred in \(55\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) oil bath for \(3\;\mathrm{{h}}\) . After collection by centrifugation and washing with ethanol several times, the precipitates were dried in a \(60\,^{\circ}\!C\) oven overnight. Using the hydrothermal method, the prepared monodispersed \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}[\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{CN})_{6}]_{2}\) PBA NC precursors were calcined and nitrided in \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) \((200\ \mathrm{sccm})/\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) (10–100 sccm) at \(450\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) with a heating rate of \(5\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\cdot\mathrm{min}^{-1}\) for \(30\ \mathrm{min}\) to \(10~\mathrm{{h}}\) to transform them into mesoporous \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{N}@\mathrm{AN-C}\) NCs. In addition, \(\mathrm{Co-N@N}\) -doped carbon (N-C) and pure \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) NCs were prepared at \(550\;^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(2\,\mathrm{{h}}\) in a \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) atmosphere and at \(500\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(2\,\mathrm{{h}}\) in an Air atmosphere, respectively.
## 2.2 Materials characterization
We observed surface morphology of the \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}[\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{CN})_{6}]_{2}\) PBA NC precursors, mesoporous \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{N}@\mathrm{AN-C},\) , pure \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4},\) and \(\mathrm{Co-N@N-C}\) NCs by field-emission scanning electron microscopy (FESEM, JEOL 7500F). We conducted high-resolution transmission electron microscopy and energy dispersive spectroscopy (EDS) mapping using a JEM2100F with accelerating voltage of \(200\;\mathrm{kV}.\) . Crystallinity and structure of the \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}[\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{CN})_{6}]_{2}\) PBA NC precursors and mesoporous \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{N}@\mathrm{AN-C}\) NCs were examined by powder X-ray diffraction (Bruker D8 FOCUS) using CuKα radiation. X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) and Fourier transform infrared (FTIR, Bruker
IFS-66/S) spectroscopy were used to analyze chemical bonding within materials. Thermogravimetry and differential thermal analysis (TG-DTA, Seiko Exstar) was conducted in \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) at a heating rate of \(5\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\cdot\mathrm{min}^{-1}\) from \(30{-}1,000\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C},\) to confirm the reaction of the \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}[\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{CN})_{6}]_{2}\) PBA NC precursors. We measured the Brunauer– Emmett–Teller specific surface areas and the Barrett-Joyner-Halenda pore-size distributions of the samples on the surface area by using a pore-size analyzer (Autosorb-iQ 2ST/MP) at \(77\,\mathrm{~K~}\) with \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) gas. The metallic element (Co) in as-prepared catalyst samples was determined by using inductively coupled plasma-optical emission spectrometry (ICP-OES, Agilent 5100). For ammonia-temperature programmed desorption \(\mathrm{(NH_{3}{\cdot}T P D)}\) analysis, all samples were preheated at \(300~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(1\,\mathrm{~h~}\) with Ar gas to clean the surface, and then, \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) was chemisorbed at \(100~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(1\,\mathrm{~h~}\) . The chemisorbed \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) was desorbed by increasing the temperature to \(900\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) (ramping rate of \(10\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\cdot\mathrm{min}^{-1}\) ), and a thermal conductivity detector detected the desorbed \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) .
## 2.3 Electrochemical measurements
The linear-sweep voltammetry with a scan rate of \(1\,\mathrm{\mV{\cdot}s^{-1}}\) and cyclic voltammetry in 1 M KOH were conducted on a CHI 660D (CH Instruments, Inc., Austin, TX) electrochemistry work station using a three-electrode cell. \(\mathrm{Hg/HgO}\) (1 M NaOH) and Pt wire electrodes were used as reference and counter electrodes, respectively. The working electrode was prepared by mixing as-prepared powder samples, Ketchen black as a conductive agent, and polyvinylidene fluoride (PVDF) as a binder (7:2:1 in weight ratio). These ingredients were added to N-methyl-2-pyrrolidone (NMP) to prepare the slurry, coated onto carbon paper ( \(1\;\mathrm{cm}\times1\) cm, Tray, Japan), and dried at \(90\,^{\circ}\!C\) in a vacuum oven overnight. Catalyst loading for as-prepared powder samples was approximately \(2.6\,\mathrm{\mg{\cdot}c m}^{-2}\) . Electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) measurements of as-prepared powder samples were conducted using a three-electrode system with frequency range \(100\;\mathrm{kHz}{-}0.1\;\mathrm{Hz},\) and amplitude of applied voltage was \(5\;\mathrm{mV}.\) The stability of mesoporous \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{N}@\mathrm{AN-C}\) NCs were conducted on WBCS-3000 (Xeno Co.) in 1 M KOH by applying a static current density of \(20\;\mathrm{mA}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}\) .
## 3 Results and discussion
## 3.1 Preparation and characterizations of 3D and mesoporous Co based electrocatalysts
The strategy used to obtain mesoporous \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{N}@\mathrm{AN-C}\) NCs for OER catalyst offered a remarkable performance to convert \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}[\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{CN})_{6}]_{2}\) PBA NCs into \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{N}@\mathrm{AN-C}\) NCs with a unique structure by annealing under a \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}/\mathrm{N}_{2}\) atmosphere at \(450\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) (Fig. 1). First, highly uniform and monodispersed \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}[\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{CN})_{6}]_{2}\) PBA NCs with a –CN link group as abundant carbon and nitrogen sources were
readily synthesized by a hydrothermal process. We used direct and one-step conversion of carbonization and nitridation to obtain mesoporous \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{N}@\mathrm{AN-C}\) NCs. Additionally, an in situ grown carbon shell could prevent aggregation of \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{N}\) nanoparticles, which could increase active sites and improve the stability of the catalyst during the OER process. Finally, we demonstrated synergy of mesoporous transition-metal nitride \(\left(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{N}\right)\) NCs and AN-C matrix to achieve a high performance of OER.
reveals high-resolution TEM images of individual \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{N}\) nanoparticles and reveals well-resolved lattice fringe of approximately \(0.180\ \mathrm{nm}\) corresponds to the d spacing of (201) crystal planes of hexagonal \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{N}\) crystal. The corresponding fast Fourier transform (FFT) pattern (inset image of Fig. 2(d)) also indicated the single crystalline nature of the \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{N}\) nanoparticles. The small particles of \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{N}\) were coated with thin amorphous N-doped carbon layers, especially. The in-situ formation of amorphous carbon on the particles leads to readily retain unique mesoporous structures without agglomeration and further crystal growth. Furthermore, energy dispersive spectroscopy (EDS) mapping result reveals relative locations of Co, N, and C elements, demonstrating uniform distribution of chemical elements in the whole mesoporous \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{N}@\mathrm{AN-C}\) NCs (Figs. 2(f)–2(h)). The crystal structure and phase deformation of the PBA NC precursors and mesoporous nitride NCs were analyzed by powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD). Corresponding PXRD pattern (Fig. 2(i) and Fig. S4 in the ESM), all the diffraction peaks indicate good accordance with cubic \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}[\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{CN})_{6}]_{2}\) PBA (JCPDS card no. 86-0501) and hexagonal crystal structure of \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{N}\) (JCPDS card no. 04-6546), and confirm successful synthesis of mesoporous \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{N}\) NCs. The Fourier transform infrared (FTIR) spectrum (see Fig. S5(a) in the ESM) of as-prepared PBA precursors exhibits strong absorption peaks at \(2,173\;\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) that can be assigned to \(-\mathrm{CN}\) stretching, attributed to the bridge of a \(\mathrm{Co}^{2+}{-}\mathrm{CN}{-}\mathrm{Co}^{3+}\) fragment, in the nanostructures of \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}[\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{CN})_{6}]_{2}\) PBA NCs [23, 24]. These peaks disappeared in the spectrum of \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{N}@\mathrm{AN-C}\) NCs, suggesting these \(-\mathrm{CN}\) groups decomposed and transformed into nitrogen-doped carbon during the thermal ammoniolysis (see Fig. S5(a) and the TG-DTA curve shown in Fig. S5(b) in the ESM) [25].
The X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) spectra illustrated the C, Co, and N in \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{N}@\mathrm{AN-C}\) NCs at \(450\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(2\,\mathrm{{h}}\) (Fig. 3(a)). The high-resolution C 1s spectrum suggested co-existence of C–C \((\mathbf{s}\mathbf{p}^{2})\) , C–N, and N–C–O bonds [26]. Additionally, the high-resolution N 1s XPS spectrum can be fitted into two parts. The peak located at \(379.74\,\mathrm{eV}\) indicate that the C–N (pyridinic-N) and \(\mathrm{Co-N}\) bonds are attributed to the N-doped carbon shell and \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{N}\) phase [27]. Especially, the peak located at \(399.39\,\mathrm{eV}\) is assigned to pyrrolic-N, suggesting a successfully converted and doped nitrogen atom was absorbed into carbon from \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}[\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{CN})_{6}]_{2}\) PBA NCs [28, 29]. Meanwhile, a representative XPS spectrum of Co 2p with two spin–orbital doublets of Co (II) and Co (III) in Fig. 3(c) shows the peaks at 779.39, 794.59,
and \(796.29\;\mathrm{eV}\) corresponding to Co (II) or Co metal, and 781.19 and \(798.09\;\mathrm{eV}\) correspond to Co (III), respectively [30, 31]. In addition, two peaks appear at 782.99 and \(800.29\;\mathrm{eV}\), which correspond to the \(\mathrm{Co-N}\) bond [32, 33]. Weak \(2\mathrm{p}_{3/2}\) satellite features were found at approximately \(784{-}790\;\mathrm{eV}\), and those of \(2\mathrm{p}_{1/2}\) satellite features were found at approximately \(802{-}806\;\mathrm{eV}\) due to the cobalt hydroxide and oxide [34–36]. XPS results implied the successful direct conversion of mesoporous cobalt nitride, N-doped carbon, and N-doped carbon surrounding \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{N}\) nanoparticles, increasing reaction sites and leading to high OER activity and better stability.
## 3.2 Electrocatalytic activity of 3D and mesoporous Co based electrocatalysts
We investigated the catalytic properties of mesoporous \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{N}@\mathrm{AN-C}\) NCs as a function of nitridation time from \(30\;\mathrm{min}\) to \(10\;\mathrm{h}\) for the OER in comparison with mesoporous \(\mathrm{Co-N}@\mathrm{N}\)-doped carbon (N–C) and \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) NCs pyrolysis product of \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}[\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{CN})_{6}]_{2}\) samples in different \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) or Air atmospheres and pristine carbon paper electrodes. The morphology and crystallinity analysis of mesoporous \(\mathrm{Co-N}@\mathrm{N-C}\) and \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) NCs were further conformed by FESEM and PXRD analysis (see Figs. S6(a)–S6(d) in the ESM). OER measurements were conducted with a catalyst loaded on carbon paper at room temperature in \(1\;\mathrm{M}\) KOH solution at a scan rate of \(1\;\mathrm{mV}{\cdot}\mathrm{s}^{-1}\) from positive to negative potential on the reversible hydrogen electrode scale. Figure 4(a) shows polarization curves for OER on six different electrodes with IR compensation including a pristine carbon-paper electrode. Especially, the mesoporous \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{N}@\mathrm{AN-C}\) NCs \((2\,\mathrm{h})\) electrode revealed the smallest overpotential, of only \(280\;\mathrm{mV}\), to achieve a current density of \(10\;\mathrm{mA}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}\), whereas mesoporous \(\mathrm{Co-N}@\mathrm{N-C}\) and \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) NCs required a relatively larger overpotential of 318 and \(335\;\mathrm{mV}\), respectively (Fig. 4(a)). This result suggested
that the intrinsic properties through composition transformation and unique 3D nanostructures of metal nitride can significantly improve catalytic activity. This value \((280\;\mathrm{mV}\) at \(10\;\mathrm{mA}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2})\) is lower than previously reported values of cobalt nitride materials \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{N}\) \((330\;\mathrm{mV}\) at \(10\;\mathrm{mA}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2})\) or cobalt nitride nanowires \((290\;\mathrm{mV}\) at \(10\;\mathrm{mA}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2})\) [37, 38]. Furthermore, mesoporous \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{N}@\mathrm{AN-C}\) NCs show much better electrochemical performance than did the non-noble transition-metal-based OER catalysts recently reported (summarized in Table S1 in the ESM). Moreover, the overpotential of \(\mathrm{RuO}_{2}\) \((262\;\mathrm{mV}\) at \(10\;\mathrm{mA}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2})\) was found to represent smaller than the as-prepared \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{N}@\mathrm{AN-C}\) NCs \((2\;\mathrm{h})\) \((280\;\mathrm{mV}\) at \(10\;\mathrm{mA}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2})\). However, the \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{N}@\mathrm{AN-C}\) NCs \((2\;\mathrm{h})\) have better electrocatalytic activity at high current density than the commercial \(\mathrm{RuO}_{2}\) catalyst. The Tafel plots of the electrochemical kinetics between the rate of OER and overpotential in Fig. 4(b) exhibit a Tafel slope of \(69.6\;\mathrm{mV}{\cdot}\mathrm{dec}^{-1}\) for \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{N}@\mathrm{AN-C}\) NCs \((2\;\mathrm{h})\), which is significantly less than that of \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) NCs \((97.1\;\mathrm{mV}{\cdot}\mathrm{dec}^{-1})\) or \(\mathrm{Co-N}@\mathrm{N-C}\) NCs \((86.4\;\mathrm{mV}{\cdot}\mathrm{dec}^{-1})\) and suggests favorable charge-transfer kinetics on \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{N}@\mathrm{AN-C}\) NCs for a better OER rate. Also, monodispersed and mesoporous \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{N}@\mathrm{AN-C}\) NC at optimized growth conditions as a function of \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) flow rates were further evaluated using polarization curves for OER catalysis with IR compensation and Tafel plots of electrodes (see Fig. S7 in the ESM). To account for the apparent difference and relationship of electrochemical active surface area (ECSA) and structure activity on electrodes, ECSA was estimated from the electrochemical double-layer capacitance \(\left(C_{\mathrm{dl}}\right)\) by cyclic voltammograms measurement (Fig. 4(d) and see Fig. S8 in the ESM) [39]. As shown in Fig. 4(c), the \(C_{\mathrm{dl}}\) of the \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{N}@\mathrm{AN-C}\) NCs \((2\;\mathrm{h})\) electrode has a \(C_{\mathrm{dl}}\) \((39\;\mathrm{mF}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2})\), whereas that of the \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) and \(\mathrm{Co-N}@\mathrm{N-C}\) electrodes were 5 and \(9\;\mathrm{mF}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}\), respectively. Capacitance of \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{N}@\mathrm{AN-C}\) NCs is approximately four times that of \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) and \(\mathrm{Co-N}@\mathrm{N-C}\), indicating that the unique
porous structures and number of effective active sites for water oxidation increased after nitridation. Moreover, the variation of \( C_{\mathrm{dl}} \) on the \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{N@AN-C}\) (30 min, 2 h, and 10 h) coincide with the BET specific surface area.
The unique mesoporous structure with interconnected particles, which can be affected by in situ growth of an amorphous carbon layer on \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{N}\) nanoparticles, offers a larger active surface area, and various electrolyte diffusion pathways lead to density of electrochemically active sites, thereby providing better electrochemical oxygen evolution reaction activity. The turnover frequency (TOF) not only determine intrinsic performance of electrocatalysts but also consider an important OER kinetic parameter [40]. The TOF values of 3D and Co based electrocatalysts for OER were also calculated against the applied overpotential at \(300\;\mathrm{mV}\) (see Fig. S9 in the ESM). As estimated, the calculated TOF of mesoporous \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{N@AN-C}\) NCs \((2\;\mathrm{h})\) for OER at \(300\;\mathrm{mV}\) is about \(2.26\times10^{-3}\;\mathrm{s}^{-1}\), which was much higher than that of \(\mathrm{Co-N@N-C}\) \((3.51\times10^{-4}\;\mathrm{s}^{-1})\) and \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) NCs \((1.17\times10^{-3}\;\mathrm{s}^{-1})\) catalysts. Moreover, to indirectly verify \(\mathrm{OH}^{-}\)affinity, the surface acidic property and possible interaction of mesoporous Co based electrocatalysts, the \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\)-temperature programmed desorption \(\left(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\right.\)-TPD) of all three materials were carried out after \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) chemisorption at \(100\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(1\,\mathrm{h}\) (see Fig. S10 in the ESM). The \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{N@AN-C}\) NCs \((2\,\mathrm{h})\) exhibited a sharp \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) desorption peak a sharp desorption peak at \(601.1\;^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) \((37.648\;\mathrm{cm}^{3}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1})\) and a broad desorption peak at approximately \(700\;^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) at surface. The \(\mathrm{Co-N@N-C}\) NCs exhibited a sharp desorption peak at \(511.5\;^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) \((25.126\;\mathrm{cm}^{3}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1})\) and a broad desorption peak at approximately \(684.6\;^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\). Moreover, the \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) NCs exhibited a sharp desorption peak at a higher temperature \((800\;^{\circ}\mathrm{C},\;43.431\;\mathrm{cm}^{3}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1})\) than the \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{N@AN-C}\) NCs \((2\;\mathrm{h})\). These results indicate that the formation of \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{N}\) and interatomic coupling of \(\mathrm{Co-N}\) changed the electrophilic properties and more electrophilic of the exposed Co surface [41, 42]. Theas results suggest that the higher intrinsic activity of \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{N@AN-C}\) NCs \((2\;\mathrm{h})\) than other catalysts was demonstrated due to the predominance of the active sites on the catalyst surface possess and synergetic interaction of the \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{N@AN-C}\) hybrid structures in OER catalysis.
Additionally, to elucidate better OER electrocatalytic efficiency for \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{N@AN-C}\) NCs, electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) was conducted to study electrode kinetics under OER by circuitmodel fitting analysis. Nyquist plots (Fig. 4(d)) reveal that the charge-transfer resistance of the \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{N@AN-C}\) NCs \((2\;\mathrm{h})\) is less than that of mesoporous \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\), which is in good agreement with the Tafel slope (Fig. 4(b)). These results suggest that \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{N@AN-C}\) NCs \((2\;\mathrm{h})\) have a faster charge-transfer process than does semiconductor \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) NCs, which could be associated with the unique electronic structure of 3D and mesoporous \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{N}\) and the N-doped carbon coating layer, which are in good agreement with excellent OER performance.
3.3 Stability test and role of the 3D and mesoporous \(\mathbf{Co}_{3}\mathbf{N}@\mathbf{AN-C}\) NCs electrocatalysts during OER
Figure 4(e) shows a chronopotentiometric curve obtained for mesoporous \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{N@AN-C}\) NCs and time dependence of overpotential at a continuous current density of \(20\;\mathrm{mA}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}\) that is almost without drastic changes for \(24\;\mathrm{h}\). Additionally, as revealed in the TEM image (inset image of Fig. 4(e)), mesoporous \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{N@AN-C}\) NCs retained their mesoporous structure without noticeable aggregation or degradation in structure. The polarization curve for mesoporous \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{N@AN-C}\) NCs revealed excellent stability and exhibit negligible degradation in stable current density \((20\;\mathrm{mA}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2})\) before and after \(24\;\mathrm{h}\) of testing (Fig. 4(f)). This observation suggests that the abundant mesoporous and unique structure of \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{N}\) NCs with N-doped carbon coating leads to sustainable large reaction sites and more durability for a long time in the process of OER.
Furthermore, we investigated the OER process and progressive change of mesoporous \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{N@AN-C}\) NCs using XRD and XPS (Figs. 5(a)–5(c)). After the OER process, the diffraction intensity of \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{N}\) peaks vanished and new diffraction peak of \(\mathrm{CoOOH}\) (JCPDS no. 71-5039) appeared (Fig. 5(a)). It was attributed to the formation and conversion of \(\mathrm{Co(O)OH}\) at the surface of cobalt nitride nanoparticles after the OER process. The highly exposed Co atoms on the surface that are oxidized easily \(\mathrm{Co(O)OH}\) compare with other Co based materials, and a further electro-oxidation-generated \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\) evolution [18]. Moreover, in comparison to the change of surface chemical state of the as-prepared \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{N@AN-C}\) NCs \((2\;\mathrm{h})\) during the OER process (Figs. 5(b) and 5(c)), the BE position Co (II) \(2\mathrm{p}_{3/2}\) and \(2\mathrm{p}_{1/2}\) in the XPS peak shows a negative shift of approximately \(0.6\;\mathrm{eV}\), which could be assigned to the formation and conversion of a higher oxidation state from \(\mathrm{Co(II)}\) to \(\mathrm{Co(III)}\) species in cobalt oxide/hydroxide by surface oxidation transformation in cobalt nitrides catalysts [43]. Moreover, the intensity of the pyridinic or cobalt nitride \((397.93\;\mathrm{eV})\) peak became observably weaker (see Fig. S11 in the ESM). The deconvoluted O 1s spectrum shows three prominent components at 529.86, 531.1, and \(532.09\;\mathrm{eV}\), which can be assigned to typical metal-oxygen bonds, hydroxyl groups, and absorbed water, respectively [44]. In addition, the N content at the surface of the \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{N@AN-C}\) after OER process drastically drops to 0.94 at.% and the O content increases to 11.23 at.% (see Table S2 in the ESM). These results further indicate the possible catalytic mechanism of cobalt nitrides that are transformed into oxides at the surface during OER process, which become efficient active sites and promote the reaction rate for OER. These results further indicate the possible catalytic mechanism of cobalt nitrides that are transformed into oxides at the surface during OER process, which become efficient active sites and promote the reaction rate for OER.
Excellent performance of the 3D and mesoporous \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{N@AN-C}\) NCs could arise from multiple synergistic effects of their unique
electrical (metal nitride and N-doped carbon) and structural properties (mesoporous). Cobalt nitrides have metallic properties, of which high electrical conductivity caused by the electron cloud overlapping causes faster charge transport to the catalyst surface, thereby giving superior OER activity. In addition, abundant exposed cobalt atoms on the surface of cobalt nitrides are oxidized easily and can transform into cobalt (oxy)hydroxide by absorption of OH- species, which provide active sites and efficient species for the more efficient OER process [35, 45]. Second, improved OER activity of the \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{N}@\mathrm{AN-C}\) NCs could contribute strongly interconnected small nanoparticles to their complex 3D mesoporous structure as building blocks, because of the in-situ growth of carbon layer, and provide high reaction surface area and structural stability. Additionally, the open structure endows various electrolyte diffusion paths and large electrode-electrolyte contact areas that facilitate large electrochemically active sites for OER.
## 4 Conclusions
In summary, 3D and mesoporous \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{N}@\mathrm{AN-C}\) NCs made from \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}[\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{CN})_{6}]_{2}\) PBA NC precursors have been successfully synthesized using a simple two-step hydrothermal method and thermal aminomolysis process as an efficient OER electrocatalyst. In situ synthesized 3D and mesoporous \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{N}@\mathrm{AN-C}\) NCs after nitridation \((450\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C},2\,\mathrm{h})\) demonstrate greater OER activity with a remarkably low Tafel plot \((69.6\mathrm{~mV}{\cdot}\mathrm{dec}^{-1})\), low overpotential of \(280~\mathrm{mV}\) at a current density of \(10\;\mathrm{mA}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}\), and excellent cycling stability in alkaline electrolytes without morphological changes, superior to most reported hierarchical structures of transition-metal nitride particles. To our knowledge, further such synthetic approaches from MOF-based materials can be conducted to explore potential application of the unique mesoporous and transition-metal nitrides-carbon hybrid materials for cost-effective and advanced energy conversion and storage.
